  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Monday)\nIssued at 114 PM CST TUE NOV 24 2026\n\nVisible and water vapor satellite imagery show a pair of troughs\nto our west: one over Texas and one over the northern Great\nPlains. The southernmost trough will bring widespread rain\nWednesday into Wednesday night, during Thanksgiving Eve travel,\nas lift and increased vertical moisture content arrive. While the\nbulk of rain will be associated with the front (on Wednesday),\nisentropic lift ahead of the front should result in spotty\nshowers for parts of central Alabama starting tonight.\nProbabilities of rain totals reaching one inch are medium to high\nfor areas northwest of the 85.\n\nAs the two troughs interact on Thanksgiving Day, a large area of\nwrap-around of rain and snow is projected to our north. Central\nAlabama will become involved within the mid-level dry slot, so\ndry conditions will prevail after the last batch of rain exits by\nearly in the morning. We'll keep an eye on locations from Highway\n278 northward (Hamilton to Centre vicinity) as that's about the\nextent of where a few wrap-around showers could occur, a low\nchance at this juncture. Variable clouds linger through the day\n(persistent cloudiness in northern Alabama vs. gradual clearing\nsouth in Alabama). A breezy/gusty west to northwest wind will add\nto already cool/chilly conditions.\n\nHigh pressure builds in for Friday and Saturday. Ensembles aren't\nclear for Sunday: some show another trough/front quickly moving\nin with widespread rain while others are either slower with the\narrival or less rainy if at all. The official forecast currently\nreflects low to medium chances for rain on Sunday.\n\n89^GSatterwhite",
